      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my sec+ and im kinda stuck on something. if we're already hashing passwords, why do we even need to add a \"salt\" to them? wouldn't the hash be enough to keep them secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, my dear friend, let me tell you a tale of the goblin world. Imagine you've got a treasure chest filled with gold coins, and you lock it with a magical spell that only you know. Now, if a sneaky goblin finds out the spell, they could easily break into your chest. But what if, before casting the spell, you sprinkle a pinch of enchanted dust on each coin? Even if the goblin figures out the spell, they can't just use the dust to duplicate your coins. The dust, or \"salt\" in our case, makes each coin unique, so the goblin can't just copy your treasure. The same goes for passwords. The hash is like the spell, but the salt makes each password unique, making it harder for goblins (or hackers) to crack them. So, you see, the salt is like the enchanted dust, adding an extra layer of security to your precious passwords.",
